{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-05-24T21:05:36.676Z","tool_name":"update_topic","tool_id":"update_topic_1779656736676_0","parameters":{"strategic_intent":"Search for and download suitable datasets for experimentation.","title":"Phase 2: Dataset Search and Download","summary":"Literature review of key papers (Janna Lu, Time-LLM, ForecastBench) has provided a nuanced view of LLM vs human performance. While LLMs excel in numerical time series (high-data) and can outperform the general human crowd in event forecasting, they still lag behind expert human forecasters. I am now transitioning to Phase 2: Dataset Search. I will look for datasets that support testing both short-term high-data scenarios (numerical time series like ETT, Weather, Electricity) and long-term low-data scenarios (Metaculus, ForecastBench, or similar event-based datasets). This will allow for empirical verification of the research hypothesis."}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-05-24T21:05:36.682Z","tool_name":"run_shell_command","tool_id":"run_shell_command_1779656736682_1","parameters":{"description":"Searching for datasets on HuggingFace.","command":"cat > find_datasets.py << 'EOF'\nfrom datasets import list_datasets, load_dataset\nimport os\n\n# Search for forecasting/time series datasets\nsearch_terms = [\"forecasting\", \"time-series\", \"metaculus\", \"forecastbench\"]\nfound_datasets = []\n\n# This is a bit slow, so I'll just look for specific known good ones first\ntarget_datasets = [\n    \"monash_tsf\", # Large collection of time series\n    \"informer\",   # ETT datasets\n    \"huggingface/time-series-transformer\", # Reference to TS datasets\n]\n\n# Better yet, let's use the datasets library to search\nprint(\"Searching for relevant datasets on HuggingFace...\")\n# Note: list_datasets() is deprecated/removed in newer versions, use huggingface_hub\nfrom huggingface_hub import list_datasets as hf_list_datasets\n\nfor term in search_terms:\n    print(f\"Searching for '{term}'...\")\n    datasets = hf_list_datasets(filter=term)\n    count = 0\n    for d in datasets:\n        print(f\"Found: {d.id}\")\n        found_datasets.append(d.id)\n        count += 1\n        if count >= 5: break\n\n# Specific datasets to download\n# 1. For short-term high-data: ETT (Electricity Transformer Temperature)\n# 2. For long-term low-data: Metaculus (if available) or similar\n\n# ETT is often in monash_tsf or specific repos\n# Let's try to load a small one for verification\ntry:\n    print(\"Loading a sample time series dataset (ETT)...\")\n    # ETTm1 is a classic benchmark\n    # dataset = load_dataset(\"thuml/ETT\", \"ETTm1\") # This might not be the right path\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f\"Error loading ETT: {e}\")\n\nEOF\n\npython find_datasets.py\n"}}
{"type":"tool_result","timestamp":"2026-05-24T21:05:38.327Z","tool_id":"update_topic_1779656736676_0","status":"success","output":"## 📂 Topic: **Phase 2: Dataset Search and Download**\n\n**Summary:**\nLiterature review of key papers (Janna Lu, Time-LLM, ForecastBench) has provided a nuanced view of LLM vs human performance. While LLMs excel in numerical time series (high-data) and can outperform the general human crowd in event forecasting, they still lag behind expert human forecasters. I am now transitioning to Phase 2: Dataset Search. I will look for datasets that support testing both short-term high-data scenarios (numerical time series like ETT, Weather, Electricity) and long-term low-data scenarios (Metaculus, ForecastBench, or similar event-based datasets). This will allow for empirical verification of the research hypothesis.\n\n> [!STRATEGY]\n> **Intent:** Search for and download suitable datasets for experimentation."}
{"type":"tool_result","timestamp":"2026-05-24T21:05:38.327Z","tool_id":"run_shell_command_1779656736682_1","status":"success"}
